From 5c29132972a35ea65941526c0ba57b18510c576c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: duncan0k <dev@ark0.si>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 22:45:08 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 79/92] rescue: vendor the ark0-rescue verifier and build it as
 a static library

The rescue lane of M4-R1 checks VOLE-in-the-head proofs (ark0-rescue
SPEC.md v0.2 revision 2, version byte 0x02, statement 0x01), whose only
implementation for a node is the Rust crate voleith. This vendors it in
src/rescue/voleith/, as it is at ark0-rescue commit f5a2bc0 (rust/,
unmodified), with the crates it depends on in src/rescue/vendor/, and
builds it from CMake into a static library that holds the verifier and
its C ABI, voleith_verify_s1, and nothing of the prover.

f5a2bc0 is the reviewed crate. The series first vendored ff4ea00, which
it contains; between the two, what the verifier computes is unchanged
but for its SHAKE256, now built in src/sym.rs on the permutation of the
keccak crate instead of the sha3 crate, and the C ABI is the same. The
re-vendoring leaves the library two dependencies, keccak and zeroize:
sha3, digest, block-buffer, crypto-common, generic-array, typenum and
version_check are no longer compiled.

- vendor/ is the output of cargo vendor --locked --versioned-dirs for the
  crate's Cargo.lock. The 40 packages that no build here compiles, the
  dev-dependencies of the crate's tests and benchmarks and the
  dependencies of getrandom on other systems, keep only their Cargo.toml
  and a checksum file that lists it, and src/lib.rs where the manifest
  names no library path (stub-vendor.py): cargo reads their manifests to
  resolve the lock file and compiles none of them. 6 packages are whole;
  637 files and 7.8 MB instead of 2,505 files; regenerating it gives the
  same tree. generic-array's src/lib.rs has CRLF line endings, so
  .gitattributes keeps that package binary for git am.
- The build is cargo build --release --offline --locked --frozen
  --no-default-features --lib for an explicit target, run by
  cargo-build.cmake so that two builds give the same bytes and nothing
  outside the tree changes them: the rustc and cargo of exactly Rust
  1.97.0 (rust-toolchain.toml), taken from the toolchain's sysroot and
  checked at configure time, with RUSTC naming that rustc; every CARGO_*,
  RUST* and job server variable of the environment removed, the names
  taken from the whole environment text by one regular expression (no
  CMake list, whose ";", "[" and "]" in a value would split or merge
  records), upper-cased on Windows, where names have no case; a new
  CARGO_HOME, and no .cargo/config.toml above the crate or the build
  directory; the crate's release profile (lto, codegen-units 1,
  panic = "unwind", which the C ABI needs to catch a panic) also given
  with --config, and the build failing if anything is compiled with
  panic=abort; every local path remapped.
- Every file of the crate and of vendor/ is a dependency, and a change
  to any of them builds the library again from nothing: cargo takes
  vendored packages for immutable and would keep what it compiled. The
  sorted list of those inputs is written, only when it changes, to a
  file both cargo commands depend on, so that a deleted file, or one
  added with an old time stamp, rebuilds the library and the tools.
- The archive is an imported target, linked explicitly into
  bitcoin_consensus and bitcoinkernel: it cannot be merged as objects. A
  shared bitcoinkernel exports none of its symbols: --exclude-libs on
  ELF, -hidden-l on macOS. A static one is installed with the archive
  next to it, as libbitcoinkernel_voleith, which libbitcoinkernel.pc
  lists in Libs.private with every system library a program that links
  the static kernel needs: the kernel's own (bcrypt on Windows), the Rust
  library's, and the C++ runtime that the C compiler does not link,
  written by cmake/module/PkgConfigLibs.cmake: options as they are,
  absolute paths escaped for the .pc syntax, names as -l<name>. On Linux
  those are the libraries the Rust standard library needs but -lgcc_s
  and -lutil: nothing in it uses libutil, and _Unwind_* comes from the
  C++ link.
- With BUILD_TESTS, a second cargo build makes the crate's command-line
  tools (feature cli) for the functional tests, which prove rescue spends
  as they run. Nothing links them, and they are not installed.

README.md records the source commit, every file's SHA-256 and the
licences. Building the node now needs cargo and rustc 1.97.0.
